{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "lem:exact-full-data-interval-calibration",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every \\(P\\in\\mathcal M_{N,T,\\mathbf n}^{\\dagger}(\\lambda)\\), the full-data interval in \\texttt{def:honest-interval} obeys\n\\[\nP\\{\\tau_n(z_0)\\in C_\\alpha\\mid E_0\\}\\ge1-\\alpha\n\\]\nwhenever its deterministic class constants and donor fold sizes are defined.  The statement is finite-sample; it uses separate bounded-summand calibration for the historical and event components and the fourth-moment donor calibration, joined only by Bonferroni.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "P",
      "M_dagger",
      "N",
      "T",
      "n_vec",
      "lambda",
      "tau_n",
      "z_0",
      "C_alpha",
      "alpha",
      "E_0"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "lem:identification-fwl",
      "lem:full-data-local-polynomial-control",
      "def:full-design-centered-class",
      "def:target",
      "def:feasible-estimator",
      "def:honest-interval",
      "def:local-modulus",
      "ass:bounded-historical-wealth",
      "ass:bounded-event-wealth",
      "ass:cross-unit-independence",
      "ass:donor-independence",
      "ass:donor-replication",
      "ass:donor-ellipticity",
      "ass:donor-fourth-moment",
      "ass:local-two-sided-modulus",
      "ass:event-full-design-mean-stability",
      "ass:historical-full-design-centering"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(\\mathscr F=\\sigma(\\mathscr X,E_0,(Z_j)_{j=1}^{N})\\), where \\(\\mathscr X\\) is the complete historical design.  Since \\(z_0=\\mathbb E[Z_j\\mid E_0]\\), adjoining \\(z_0\\) does not enlarge \\(\\sigma(E_0)\\).  Donor independence and the two full-design restrictions give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[\\varepsilon_{sti}\\mid\\mathscr F]=0,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E[B_{si}^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\mid\\mathscr F]\n=\\mathbb E[B_{si}^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\mid E_0].\n\\]\nConditional cross-unit independence makes the unit-level pairs consisting of a complete historical error path and an event observation independent across \\((s,i)\\), conditional on \\(\\mathscr F\\).\n\nOn a good sample, polynomial reproduction and Taylor's formula, as proved in \\texttt{lem:full-data-local-polynomial-control}, give\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\sum_{s,t,i}\\pi_s\\bar a_{sti}m_s(X_{st})\n-\\frac1{K_D}\\sum_{s,k}\\pi_sm_s(\\widetilde z_{sk})\n\\right|\\le A_L.\n\\]\nThe identifying equality in \\texttt{lem:identification-fwl} and the full-data estimator identity therefore yield\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\widehat\\tau-\\tau_n(z_0)\n={}&\n\\sum_{s,i}\\frac{\\pi_s}{n_s}\n\\left\\{B_{si}^{\\mathrm{obs}}\n-\\mathbb E[B_{si}^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\mid E_0]\\right\\}\\\\\n&-\\sum_{s,i}\\pi_s\\sum_t\\bar a_{sti}\\varepsilon_{sti}\n-\\mathcal B_L-\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{don}},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere \\(|\\mathcal B_L|\\le A_L\\) and\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{don}}\n=\\frac1{K_D}\\sum_k\\sum_s\\pi_s\n\\{m_s(\\widetilde z_{sk})-m_s(z_{0s})\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor completeness, the bounded-sum inequality used twice follows from convexity.  If \\(W\\in[a,b]\\) and \\(\\mathbb E[W]=0\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[e^{tW}]\n\\le\\exp\\{t^2(b-a)^2/8\\}.\n\\]\nMultiplying this bound for conditionally independent summands and applying Markov's inequality to both signs gives\n\\[\nP\\left\\{\\left|\\sum_\\nu W_\\nu\\right|>x\\,\\middle|\\,\\mathscr F\\right\\}\n\\le2\\exp\\left\\{-\\frac{2x^2}{\\sum_\\nu(b_\\nu-a_\\nu)^2}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nFor a historical unit, the centered contribution\n\\[\nW_{si}^{\\mathrm{hist}}\n=\\pi_s\\sum_t\\bar a_{sti}\\varepsilon_{sti}\n\\]\nhas conditional range length at most\n\\[\nB\\pi_s\\sum_t|\\bar a_{sti}|,\n\\]\nbecause \\(0\\le Q_{sti}\\le B\\) and its conditional mean is subtracted.  Consequently\n\\[\nP\\!\\left\\{\n\\left|\\sum_{s,i}W_{si}^{\\mathrm{hist}}\\right|>R_{\\mathrm{hist}}\n\\,\\middle|\\,\\mathscr F\\right\\}\\le\\alpha_{\\mathrm{hist}}.\n\\]\nLikewise each centered event summand has range length \\(B\\pi_s/n_s\\), so\n\\[\nP\\!\\left\\{\n\\left|\\sum_{s,i}\\frac{\\pi_s}{n_s}\n\\{B_{si}^{\\mathrm{obs}}-\\mathbb E[B_{si}^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\mid E_0]\\}\\right|\n>R_{\\mathrm{evt}}\\,\\middle|\\,\\mathscr F\\right\\}\n\\le\\alpha_{\\mathrm{evt}}.\n\\]\nThese are separate probability bounds.  No independence between the event and historical variable of the same unit has been used.\n\nFor the donor term, independence and standardization give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[Y_j\\mid E_0]=0,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E[Y_jY_j^\\top\\mid E_0]=I_D.\n\\]\nExpanding the fourth power of the norm of the standardized complementary-fold sum and retaining the index patterns in which every index occurs at least twice gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\n\\left\\|m_k^{-1/2}\\sum_{j\\notin k}Y_j\\right\\|_2^4\n\\,\\middle|\\,E_0\\right]\n\\le\n\\frac{B}{m_k}\n+\\left(1-\\frac1{m_k}\\right)(D^2+2D)\n=M_4(m_k).\n\\]\nMarkov's inequality, the projection and ellipticity conversion, and the definition of \\(q_k,\\delta_k\\) imply\n\\[\nP\\!\\left\\{\n\\left\\|\\Sigma^{-1/2}\n\\{\\operatorname{vec}((\\widetilde z_{sk})_s)-z_0\\}\\right\\|_2\n>\\delta_k\\,\\middle|\\,E_0\\right\\}\n\\le\\frac{\\alpha_{\\mathrm{don}}}{K_D}.\n\\]\nA union bound over \\(k\\), followed by the upper local modulus on\n\\(\\max_k\\delta_k\\le\\delta_0\\), gives\n\\[\nP\\{|\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm{don}}|>A_{\\mathrm{don}}\\mid E_0\\}\n\\le\\alpha_{\\mathrm{don}}.\n\\]\nBonferroni over the historical, event, and donor events now gives coverage at least\n\\[\n1-\\alpha_{\\mathrm{hist}}-\\alpha_{\\mathrm{evt}}-\\alpha_{\\mathrm{don}}\n=1-\\alpha\n\\]\non the good sample.  On the bad sample \\(C_\\alpha=[-B,B]\\), and \\(\\tau_n(z_0)\\in[-B,B]\\) by bounded potential wealth.  Thus the same coverage bound holds unconditionally given \\(E_0\\).",
